# Memory collisions, second batch: `memory_collision` to 50 cases (34 cases).
#
# The first 16 are in `memory.jsonl` and stay untouched.
#
# NOTE, 2026-09-24: `benches/memory-collision-toward-branch.jsonl` is a DIFFERENT second
# batch for this point (52 cases, ids mc-101..mc-152) that appeared in the working tree from
# outside this run. The two do not overlap in ids and both can be run. This file is the one
# `fixtures/fourth-batch.jsonl` recorded and `docs/results/2026-09-24-fourth-batch/` reports,
# so deleting it makes that published run unreproducible; if the other batch is meant to
# replace it, re-record and re-publish rather than removing this file quietly.
#
# WHY THIS POINT NEEDED ITS OWN BATCH, and why adding cases to `memory-b.jsonl` would not
# have done. `docs/results/2026-09-24-fifty` said it in one line and then left it: this point
# has four labels rather than two, an abstention band, and **a `duplicate` branch that had
# never fired at the shipped thresholds in any run**. A branch that has never fired is not a
# measurement, it is an untested code path with a test suite around it.
#
# The four labels are not four opinions. Three of them are decided by the harness, not by the
# model, and getting that straight is what the cases below are for:
#
#   duplicate  `adds_nothing >= act`. The new fact restates the stored one. Nothing to store,
#              nothing to ask a person.
#   keep_both  `contradicts < act`. No contradiction, and the new fact adds something.
#   replace    contradicts AND the harness knows the new one is newer (`newer=true`).
#   flag       contradicts and the harness does NOT know which is newer (`newer=null`).
#
# `newer` is never asked of the decider. This model class reads dates as text - its own card
# says so and paper Section 5.3 measured it - so recency comes from timestamps the harness
# already has. The same pair of facts therefore has a different correct label depending on
# `newer` alone, and **eight of these cases are triples of exactly that shape**: the same
# `new` and `stored` with `newer` true, false and null, labelled replace, keep_both and flag.
# Nothing about the text distinguishes them. A point that scores well on those has understood
# that it is not being asked who is right.
#
# THE TRAP FAMILY, which is the one the sibling point already got wrong once. Corroboration
# is not contradiction: the same figure attributed to a second body is `duplicate` when it
# adds no new source and `keep_both` when the second source is itself the new information.
# `mc-19` and `mc-20` are that pair, and the difference is whether `stored` already names its
# source.
#
# Annotator 1 (the author) labelled every case before any decision was run. Annotator 2 is a
# separate generative model, blind to both; agreement is in the results directory.
